Z||000000||jch82||10-18-2001||10:56 PM||||I want to get my newly acquired 84SE on the road ASAP but my problem is that the driver side headlight won't turn on. I bought a new headlamp so that isnt the problem. The only way I can get both headlights to be on is if my brights are one. It's like something isn't hooked up for the low beams, only highbeams.....I really need to get this fixed!! Thanks!!!<P>Josh  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/jch82flag.jpg"> ||||reg||4
Z||000001||SLYGUY||10-18-2001||11:29 PM||||If you only had both headlights it with the high beams I would say simply change the headlight(s). Since you did that and still have the same problem, I'd either take the new one back and demand an exchange (difficult with elec. items) or swap the headlights and see if the one from the other side acts the same way when sitting in the offending bucket.<P>Let us know what you find out.<P>Sly||||reg||1
Z||000002||Bill Osmer||10-19-2001||02:07 PM||||Many times during the life of a 15-year old car, electrical connectors (the metal parts inside the plastic shells) will become bent to the point they no longer make proper contact. Add to the possibility of a difficult connector (like some headlight changeovers I've done), a bent pin will occur and the light will appear not to work. I usually try to verify whether it's the light or the connector by taking an ohmmeter (or continuity checker) to the headlight to verify that both filaments (your headlight has 2 - high and low) are still intact. If it is, then there is a high liklihood of the connector being "bad". What I would do next, is to verify that the 12 volts actually "shows" up in the connector by measuring the appropriate pins for voltage. If there's not good voltage (you'll have to try BOTH the low beam and the high beams to do a complete test), then the problem is elsewhere down the wiring (and the problem becomes more of a challenge).<P>------------------<BR>Bill Osmer<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/forumcardsig2.jpg"> ||||reg||4
Z||000003||falconhulk||10-19-2001||03:01 PM||||Check the connector with a Voltmeter to see if its getting power (which I bet its not)  follw this back and you will either find a bad/falling apart connector or a bad wire.  Pretty easy fix.  You could have much worse problems look on the bright side.||||reg||1
